---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="In the worked budget here, ten 2,000-token tool results fill the entire 20,000-token allowance. Ten 8,000-token results exceed it fourfold. Set the return contract before the agent starts browsing.">

<p>An agent asks a short question and receives a long result. Repeat that across a few tools and the return traffic can become the main part of the request. A context plan that counts only the user's instructions misses the material the application adds after each operation.</p>
<p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>tool-call handling guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes returning client-tool content in tool-result blocks. We suggest giving each tool a return contract: what evidence it supplies, how much it may return and where the fuller record can be retrieved if needed.</p>
<h2>Allocate the space before filling it</h2>
<p>Take a hypothetical request budget of 32,000 tokens. Reserve 4,000 for fixed input and 8,000 for output. That leaves 20,000 for the ten tool results in this example. There is no other history or hidden overhead in the calculation.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Tokens per assumed result","Ten results, tokens","Share of 20,000-token allowance"]} rows={examples['tool-results-context-budget']} caption="Synthetic text budget. Ten results each included once. Fixed instructions, output reserve and other overhead are accounted for only as stated." />
<p>Ten results at 500 tokens occupy a quarter of the allowance. At 2,000 tokens they fill it. At 8,000 they require four times the space available. These figures are assumed lengths, not observations about any tool or model.</p>
<h2>Ask for evidence with a retrieval route</h2>
<p>A useful result can include the relevant passage, its source identifier, a location and an explicit indication that more content exists. That gives the agent a way to request the missing detail rather than forcing every result to contain the entire source.</p>
<p>Do not silently truncate the part that establishes a conclusion. If a tool returns a count and a few example rows, say which is which. If it returns only the beginning of a file, mark the result incomplete. The model should not have to infer that a cleanly formatted answer omits most of the evidence.</p>
<p>For a search tool, retain enough context to distinguish a title match from supporting text. For a build tool, preserve the failing command and relevant error lines rather than an unexplained success flag. Choose the contract from the decisions the agent must make next.</p>
<h2>Keep full records outside the prompt when appropriate</h2>
<p>Store the fuller output in an authorised location and pass a stable reference with the excerpt. Apply the same access controls to retrieval as to the original tool. A short prompt does not justify making a private log public.</p>
<p>When a result is a summary, label it as one and keep its source. Test questions that depend on details likely to be omitted. A smaller return is an observable saving in request size; preserving the task's required information needs a separate acceptance check.</p>
<p>Version the result format. Changing a tool from full text to selected fields changes what the agent can see. Save that version beside the model and prompt so a later quality change can be traced to the right component.</p>
<h2>Account for replay as well as the first inclusion</h2>
<p>The table counts each result once. If your application resends prior tool results on later turns, add that repeated input to the job's bill. Keep per-request token usage so that one large returned file does not disappear inside an average.</p>
<p>Before removing old results, define which facts the agent must retain and how it can recover the original evidence. A continuation summary should not become the sole authority for a decision that requires checking a source passage.</p>
<p>Record returned tokens, included tokens and accepted task outcomes for a trial. Then reduce the largest avoidable returns first and rerun the difficult cases. The aim is enough evidence to finish the job, with the amount of text made visible and bounded.</p>
<PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
